// **the refusal a template used to escape.** the three nullability forms decide their soundness
// precondition with AST::is_certainly_present, and inside a template the operand is a bare `T` - which
// that predicate deliberately answers "ask again later" for, because judging it at parse time is a
// round too early. nothing re-asked, so a `guard` over a `T` that substituted to `int32` compiled and
// ran, with an else arm that was dead code and a diagnostic the language promises never firing.
//
// AST::TypeChecker is the second asker now, against the same wording - the parser keeps its check for
// the non-generic spelling, where the message is better located

function firstOr<T>(T $maybe, T $fallback) : T
{
    T $v = guard $maybe else { return $fallback; }
    return $v;
}

echo firstOr(5, 9);
